<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.2.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00081</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.2.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>165f0e46-e7b7-4178-839d-c28dc2954d9e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">
      <exercise id="b491d30f-059b-4964-a89b-9781c582952c" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Translate to an equation: Which equation means nine more than x is equal to 52?</para>
          <para id="para-00002">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>52</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>52</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>52</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>52</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00007">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> + 9 = 52.</para>
          <para id="para-00009">Nine more than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> means that 9 is added to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> Equal to 52 means that we take whatever expression that is in front of 52 and set them equal to each other. Equal to 52 means that we take whatever expression that is in front of 52 and set them equal to each other. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>52</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>52</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_22b3e07c-7dc1-4a38-b608-7a666569a4cd" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00010">Translate to an equation: Which equation means ten less than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is −14?</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00016">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00017">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Ten less than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> means that 10 is subtracted from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> The word is, is the same as “equal to”. This means we take whatever expression that is in front of -14 and set them equal to each other. The word is, is the same as “equal to”. This means we take whatever expression that is in front of -14 and set them equal to each other. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="abd594a7-8217-4e2f-b396-f65eedf94e00" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00019">Translate to an equation: Which equation means the sum of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and −30 is 40?</para>
          <para id="para-00020">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00025">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00026">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00027">The sum of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and -30 means that you add them together. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> The word is, is the same as “equal to”. This means we take whatever expression that is in front of 40 and set them equal to each other. The word is, is the same as “equal to”. This means we take whatever expression that is in front of 40 and set them equal to each other.</para>
          <para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="d3f034b8-6d94-4cda-8653-d0ea84422be3" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00029">Translate to an equation: For a family birthday dinner, Celeste bought a turkey that weighed 5 pounds less than the one she bought for Thanksgiving. The birthday turkey weighed 16 pounds. Which equation represents how much the Thanksgiving turkey weighed?</para>
          <para id="para-00030">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00035">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00036">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00037">The important facts from the problem are that the weight of the birthday turkey is 16 pounds and that it is 5 pounds less than the Thanksgiving turkey. Choose a variable, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, to represent the Thanksgiving turkey. Choose a variable, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, to represent the Thanksgiving turkey. The birthday turkey was 5 pounds less than the one she bought for Thanksgiving. The birthday turkey was 5 pounds less than the one she bought for Thanksgiving. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> We also know the birthday turkey is 16 pounds. So they can be set equal to each other. We also know the birthday turkey is 16 pounds. So they can be set equal to each other. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a1940752-9c3b-4a6b-b93c-0c93867eb450" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00038">Translate to an equation: Which equation represents 45% of 120?</para>
          <para id="para-00039">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>45</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.45</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>45</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00044">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.4.</para>
          <para id="para-00045">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.45</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00046">45% is equivalent to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>45</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 0.45. The word “of” means that you need to multiply the two numbers together. The word “of” means that you need to multiply the two numbers together. .45 times 120 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.45</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> .45 times 120 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.45</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>120</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_0cbf272c-f4ba-47be-b722-0c6b17eb0acb" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00047">Translate to an equation: 250% of 65 can also be written as:</para>
          <para id="para-00048">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>65</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>65</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>65</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>250</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>65</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00053">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.4.</para>
          <para id="para-00054">The correct answer is x = 2.5(65).</para>
          <para id="para-00055">250% is equivalent to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>250</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 2.5 <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>250</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 2.5 The word “of” means that you need to multiply the two numbers together. The word “of” means that you need to multiply the two numbers together. 2.5 times 65 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>65</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> 2.5 times 65 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>65</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_4741cdce-5459-483b-9ce8-6a65c3a273db" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00056">Translate to an equation: Geneva treated her parents to dinner at their favorite restaurant. The bill was $74.25. Geneva wants to leave 16% of the total bill as a tip. How much should the tip be?</para>
          <para id="para-00057">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>74.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>74.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1.6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>74.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>74.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00062">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.4.</para>
          <para id="para-00063">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>74.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00064">The tip is a percentage of the bill so 16% of 74.25. 16% is equivalent to 16% is equivalent to <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 0.16 <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>16</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> or 0.16 The word “of” means that you need to multiply the two numbers together. The word “of” means that you need to multiply the two numbers together. 0.16 times 74.25 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>74.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> 0.16 times 74.25 or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0.16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>74.25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="dc4e400d-0e2a-4fbd-b9bb-6098f4f99c4b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00065">Large cheese pizzas cost $5 each, and large one-topping pizzas cost $6 each. Choose the equation that represents the total cost, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> large cheese pizzas and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> large one-topping pizzas.</para>
          <para id="para-00066">Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para><para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00071">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00072">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00073">Our equation in words would be that total cost is going to equal (the cost of one cheese pizza times the number of cheese pizzas purchased) plus (the cost of one one-topping pizza times the number of one-topping pizzas purchased).</para>
          <para id="para-00074">Substitute in the variables and prices that are given in the problem. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>T</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_857c230c-f428-4b8a-8086-fa1a7d82df3f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00075">Jada plans to serve milk and healthy cookies for a book club meeting. She is preparing 12 ounces of milk and 4 cookies per person. Including herself, there are 15 people in the club. A package of cookies contains 24 cookies and costs $4.50. A 1-gallon jug of milk contains 128 ounces and costs $3. Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of people in the club, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the ounces of milk, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent the number of cookies, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> represent Jada’s budget in dollars.</para>
          <para id="para-00076">Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> of the equations that could represent the quantities and constraints in this situation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00077">Try again. Revisit activity 1.2.3.</para>
          <para id="para-00078">The correct answers are: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4.50</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00079">The three equations that could represent the quantities and constraints in this situation are:</para>
          <para id="para-00080"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, represents the amount of milk that needs to be purchased is the amount each person will receive multiplied by the number of people in the club. This solution only works if there are 15 people in the club.</para>
          <para id="para-00081"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, represents the number of cookies that each person will receive times the variable <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> which represents the number of people in the club equals the number of cookies that need to be purchased. This solution can work for any amount of club members.</para>
          <para id="para-00082"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4.50</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, represents Jada’s budget. The number of 1-gallon jugs of milk she needs times the cost of each jug, 2(3). Plus the number of packages of cookies she needs times the cost of each package, 3(4.50). She needs 12(15)=180 ounces of milk. She will purchase <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>180</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>128</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1.41</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> gallon jugs, but since she can’t buy .41 of a jug, she will have to buy 2 of them at $3.00 each. . She needs 4(15)=60 cookies. She has to purchase <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>60</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>24</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> packages, but since she can‚t buy .5 of a package, she will have to buy 3 of them at $4.50 each. This solution can only work if she does not need more that 180 ounces of milk or 60 cookies and if the jugs of milk cost exactly $3.00 and the packages of cookies cost exactly $4.50.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>